<?xml version="1.0"?>
<model>
  <name>fpv_bird_decoy</name>
  <version>1.0</version>
  <sdf version="1.9">model.sdf</sdf>
  <author>
    <name>Interceptor Simulation (bird-rejection decoy, ADR-0035)</name>
    <email>emersonium2@gmail.com</email>
  </author>
  <description>
    Bird decoy for the drones-and-birds Monte-Carlo gate scaffolded by
    scripts/bird_mc_harness.py (docs/bird_discrimination_design.md S6.2,
    docs/decisions.md ADR-0035). A small elongated fuselage plus two flat
    wing planes in a shallow glide dihedral, sized ~0.43 m wingtip to
    wingtip (inside the design doc's 0.3-0.5 m "matched pixel footprint"
    band, so it is not trivially separable from models/fpv_target by size
    alone), colored dull brown/grey and carrying no AprilTag or fiducial,
    no 4-arm quad frame, and no prop discs or red "enemy" motor accents --
    visually and structurally distinct from models/fpv_target and
    models/fpv_target_markerless. Like those models it is static with no
    collision and no inertia, so it is purely visual and would be driven
    kinematically by a mover process (gz-transport set_pose) once wired
    into a world; it is not yet referenced by any world. This is the
    PLUMBING-tier decoy from the design doc's two-tier framing (S6.2): it
    exercises interlock wiring and stands in for the adversarial
    straight-glide case, but has no flapping actor, so a green gate
    against it is interlock-wiring validated, never bird-rejection
    validated (S6.4). Static-checked with `gz sdf --check` only; live-spawn
    and detection validation are deferred to when this decoy is actually
    wired into a world for a real Monte-Carlo run.
  </description>
</model>
